WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.557
JPA is a persistence specification; Hibernate is an implementation.

00:00:04.707 --> 00:00:09.615
An entity moves through transient, managed, detached, and removed states.

00:00:09.765 --> 00:00:13.570
A persistence context tracks managed entities and their identities.

00:00:13.720 --> 00:00:17.995
Changes to managed state can be detected and synchronized to SQL during

00:00:17.995 --> 00:00:18.351
flush.

00:00:18.501 --> 00:00:22.854
Flush sends changes to the database but does not necessarily commit the

00:00:22.854 --> 00:00:23.216
transaction.

00:00:23.366 --> 00:00:25.732
A detached entity is no longer tracked.

00:00:25.882 --> 00:00:30.316
Merging detached state copies it into a managed instance; callers should use

00:00:30.316 --> 00:00:31.424
the returned instance.

00:00:31.574 --> 00:00:37.922
Entity identity, generated IDs, equality, and lazy proxies require deliberate design.

00:00:38.072 --> 00:00:43.606
Use protected no-argument constructors and mapping annotations consistent with the selected access

00:00:43.606 --> 00:00:44.067
strategy.

00:00:44.217 --> 00:00:48.655
InventoryService loads a managed Product under a lock and calls reserve, changing

00:00:48.655 --> 00:00:49.764
its stock field.

00:00:49.914 --> 00:00:53.805
It does not need an explicit product save for dirty checking to

00:00:53.805 --> 00:00:55.751
persist that change at transaction completion.

00:00:55.901 --> 00:01:00.267
The reservation insert and product update participate in the same transaction.

00:01:00.417 --> 00:01:04.319
The rollback test proves a failed transaction discards a renamed product.

00:01:04.469 --> 00:01:09.419
Load an entity inside a transaction, modify it, flush, then roll back.

00:01:09.569 --> 00:01:13.721
Repeat with an entity loaded outside the transaction and explain the difference.

00:01:13.871 --> 00:01:17.154
Observe SQL with logging enabled only for the local lab.

00:01:17.304 --> 00:01:21.504
A managed change is synchronized on flush but remains subject to rollback.

00:01:21.654 --> 00:01:26.548
A detached object’s changes are not tracked until explicitly reattached through an

00:01:26.548 --> 00:01:27.363
appropriate operation.

00:01:27.513 --> 00:01:31.661
Do not equate save, flush, and commit; they have different meanings.

00:01:31.812 --> 00:01:32.483
Question.

00:01:32.633 --> 00:01:34.180
What is the first-level cache?

00:01:34.330 --> 00:01:34.906
Answer.

00:01:35.056 --> 00:01:40.137
The persistence context’s identity map and managed entity state, scoped to that

00:01:40.137 --> 00:01:40.560
context.

00:01:40.710 --> 00:01:41.381
Question.

00:01:41.531 --> 00:01:43.314
Does flush make a change durable?

00:01:43.464 --> 00:01:44.040
Answer.

00:01:44.190 --> 00:01:45.289
Not by itself.

00:01:45.439 --> 00:01:50.298
Durability follows successful transaction commit according to the database guarantees.
